{"original": "When autoclass_content='both', get_doc returns list of [class_doc_lines, __init__doc_lines]. Old _find_signature matches __init__ from docstrings[1]. Our loop iterates docstrings; first docstrings[0] (class doc) has no matching sig line, so continue; then docstrings[1] (__init__ doc) matches `__init__(a1,...)`. But our new code starts with `line_idx = 0` and `while line_idx < len(doclines):` — the first line might be empty string because docstring starts with newline. Let's check: `lines = F2.__init__.__doc__.split('\n')` -> first element is '' (empty). The while loop would break immediately because `py_ext_sig_re.match('')` returns None. So it misses the signature.
Problem: original code uses `doclines[0]` which would be '' and would `continue` (no match). However old code worked because doclines[0] was empty string, match fails, continue; then for second docstrings (__init__), doclines[0] is '' again and continues, so it doesn't find the signature.

Why did the original test pass? Let me check test lines more carefully.", "A": "autoclass_content='both' causes get_doc to return [class_doc, init_doc]. Old _find_signature checks only doclines[0] per docstring; new code scans all lines via while loop. Suspected failure: if init docstring's split yields '' as first element, while loop fails sig match on empty, increments, but signature may be on later line; loop would find it then. However old code would miss it entirely (only checks line 0). Since test passed, first line cannot be empty. Thus the empty-first-line hypothesis is invalid. Need to re-examine actual test lines for the real cause.", "B": "autoclass_content='both' makes get_doc return list [class_doc_lines, __init__doc_lines]; old _find_signature matches __init__ from docstrings[1]; iterating, docstrings[0] (class doc) has no sig line, continue; docstrings[1] (__init__ doc) matches. New code sets line_idx=0 and while line_idx<len(doclines); first line may be empty because __init__ docstring starts with newline (`F2.__init__.__doc__.split('\n')` first element is ''), so py_ext_sig_re.match('') returns None and while breaks, missing signature. Original code used doclines[0], which is '' and continues on no match; but old code worked: for docstrings[0], doclines[0]='' match fails, continue; for docstrings[1] (__init__), doclines[0]='' again, continue, so signature not found there either. Unresolved: why original test passed. Next: inspect test lines more carefully."}
